# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-start-engine-malformed-handler-case — AutoCAD start-engine crashes with "ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE is undefined"
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: feature-alfe-on-error

* Symptom

Driving the AutoCAD backend in automation mode — even just staging it with
--print-command, which launches no CAD — crashes:

#+begin_example
$ alfe -norc --autocad --cad acad-2022 --mode automation --dwg empty.dwg --print-command
alfe: The function ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE is undefined.
#+end_example

Reproduced on a real Windows + AutoCAD 2022 host (clautolisp-1.9.3). It is NOT
the missing-asset bug ([[alfe-install-missing-cad-runtime-lsp]]): it persists
with $ALFE_BOOTSTRAP_LSP / $ALFE_RUNTIME_LSP exported.

* Cause — a malformed HANDLER-CASE in START-ENGINE

=backend-autocad.lisp='s =%start-engine= wraps its body in

: (handler-case (let* (…) …)
:   (alfe.error:backend-error (probe) (error probe))
:   (error (probe) (error 'backend-bootstrap-error … probe …)))

but the =let*= body was missing ONE closing paren (introduced when the
=started-ok= / =unwind-protect= engine-reaping guard was copied over from the
BricsCAD backend — "The BricsCAD backend has had this guard; AutoCAD had not").
So the two intended handler CLAUSES were read as the last two FORMS of the
=let*= body instead. At that point =(alfe.error:backend-error (probe) …)= is a
function call, =(probe)= calls the (undefined) function PROBE, and
=alfe.error:backend-error= / the bound condition variable =probe= are undefined
too.

The compiler said so all along — building the system emits:

: undefined variable: ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE   (at (ERROR PROBE))
: undefined function: ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE   (at (PROBE))
: Undefined functions: ALFE.ERROR:BACKEND-ERROR, ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE

— warnings nobody had been reading, because no test exercises the AutoCAD
start-engine / automation-staging path (the OS gate makes it signal
:unsupported-os off Windows, so a test must mock the Windows environment the
=autocad-discover-windows-*= tests already do).

The BricsCAD backend's identical handler-case is correctly parenthesised, which
is why only AutoCAD was affected.

* Fix (clautolisp 1.9.6, branch feature-alfe-on-error)

- backend-autocad.lisp: add the missing =)= that closes the =let*= body before
  the handler clauses, and drop the compensating extra =)= at the end of the
  defun. Verified structurally (the two clauses now sit at handler-case
  clause depth) and by rebuild: the ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE and
  ALFE.ERROR:BACKEND-ERROR undefined warnings are gone; autolisp-front-end
  test suite 620/620 green.
- The bug surfaced while adding =--on-error= to alfe (that RUN change forced a
  recompile whose warnings exposed it).

* Acceptance criteria

- [X] Building autolisp-front-end no longer warns about undefined
  ALFE.BACKEND.AUTOCAD::PROBE / ALFE.ERROR:BACKEND-ERROR.
- [X] Full autolisp-front-end suite green.
- [ ] A regression test for the AutoCAD automation *staging* path
  (print-command-plan --autocad --mode automation) under a mocked Windows
  environment, asserting it captures a launch command / signals a proper
  backend condition rather than UNDEFINED-FUNCTION. Tracked as the "missing
  tests" note in [[alfe-release-ships-stale-image]] (whose stale-image
  hypothesis this bug supersedes — the crash is a source defect, not an old
  image; a fresh 1.9.3 build crashes the same way).
